this installation by nigerian born  artist Oh Lonnie Rashida India is made  from everyday materials such as  cardboard fabric and plastic the work is  an existential reflection of  post-colonial Africa combined with  political and environmental issues it's  a vivid complex creation representing  human interconnectedness the artist who  lives in the United States says he's  also interested in questions about  African self representation how can we  find a way to poetry to sell African as  we really are not what people portray  toes so at this time my one of my  website I'm presenting a disease vision  is called portrait the work of victory  he common or another nigerian artist  deals with the stark changes in his  country he grew up in a society largely  influenced by mythology and religion his  installation is a kind of prayer room  that recreates the world of his  childhood you know places like this they  are all going esting now you probably  won't find them anymore but I was lucky  enough as it kids to find places like  this where people could go for prayers  could good for divination could good for  prophecy you know so that is what I've  recreated here and the sculpted  paintings are representation of people  that go in there the kind of clothes  they would weird kind of colors that is  vibrant as traditions give way to  technology some old ways of life are  dying out while new lifestyles are still  being defined these are the kinds of  questions about transition posed by the  artist at the dock art biennale in  Senegal
 

